Right. You mean the whole buffet of “wares” as lenses: not just “is this hardware or software?”, but “which parts of a person’s life are involved, how do they interact, and at what scale?” Then repeat that map for relationships and groups.

That fits a well-established way of thinking about mental health: biological, psychological, family, community, and structural factors can combine and affect one another. The Wares framework could make those layers easier to see and talk about.

A Wares map across scales

Lens	Within one person	Between people or in a group
Wetware	Brain, nervous system, body, sensory differences, illness or pain	People’s bodies respond to each other: calming, tension, fatigue, safety cues
Powerware	Sleep, energy, pain load, attention, time	Shared capacity: caregiving load, understaffing, money, time, recovery
Firmware	Learned reflexes, protective habits, early expectations	Family scripts, team habits, cultural patterns passed along
Processware	Attention, emotion, thought loops, coping, choice	Communication patterns, conflict cycles, group decision habits
Dataware	Memories, expectations, learned associations	Shared history, promises, family stories, records, rumors
Interfaceware	How a person notices, names, and expresses experience	Whether people can hear, understand, and respond to one another
Networkware	Felt connection, trust, isolation, support	Who is included, excluded, relied upon, or able to offer care
Orgware / governanceware	Personal routines, boundaries, priorities, access to help	Roles, workload, authority, rules, services, and who gets a say
Meaningware	Identity, values, purpose, dignity, hope	Belonging, shared purpose, stigma, group identity, collective hope

The same lens can recur at multiple scales, but the mechanism changes. A person’s learned threat response and a family’s conflict script may resemble each other as patterns; they are not literally the same thing. That distinction keeps the map useful instead of turning people into machines.

The part–whole idea

A person is a whole made of interacting processes. That person is also part of a couple, family, team, community, and society. Each whole has its own state and pressures; each person both affects and is affected by it.

For example:

A workplace has chronic understaffing (group powerware) and a norm that asking for help looks weak (group firmware / meaningware). One worker gets less sleep (personal powerware), becomes more vigilant (wetware), worries about mistakes (processware), and stops speaking up (interfaceware). Coworkers read the silence as disengagement, so they offer less support (networkware). The team’s original conditions are now being reinforced by individual coping patterns.

That map doesn’t say the worker is “the problem.” It shows how strain can travel through a system and create a feedback loop. WHO guidance likewise treats mental health as shaped by interacting individual, family, community, and structural factors.

How Pantheon could use it to help

I’d make a Mental Health Ware Map a guided conversation tool:

1. Start with the experience: What feels difficult or is getting harder? Use the person’s own words; don’t start by assigning a label.
2. Mark the relevant wares: Which layers seem involved? Which are resources or strengths?
3. Place them at the right scales: Is this happening within the person, between people, in a group, or in the surrounding conditions?
4. Draw the connections: What seems to worsen or ease what? Mark observations separately from guesses.
5. Look for leverage: Which small change might reduce pressure—more recovery, clearer roles, better communication, safer connection, practical support, clinical care, or a change in the environment?
6. Revisit the map: Did anything change? Did relief in one place create a new problem somewhere else?

The key insight is that the place distress shows up may not be the place with the best leverage. Worry may show up in a person’s thoughts while the biggest available change is a group’s workload or an institution’s access rules. Conversely, improving the environment may help but not replace individual care someone wants or needs.

This should be a shared language and reflection aid, not a diagnostic taxonomy. It could help a person explain their experience to someone they trust or bring a more organized picture to a clinician. The Pantheon contribution would be connecting the parts without flattening them: what is observed, at which level, through which pathway, with what uncertainty, and where a humane change might help.